{"study_accession":"PRJEB75741","secondary_study_accession":"ERP160301","title":"B cell receptor in MBL","description":"The central role of the B cell receptor (BcR) in the development of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is widely acknowledged. However, understanding its precise significance in the early stages of CLL, such as monoclonal B cell lymphocytosis (MBL), remains a challenge. In this study, we conducted a thorough examination of the BcR in both low-count (LC-MBL, n=16) and high-count MBL (HC-MBL, n=14) cases, comparing them to CLL.\nHC-MBL closely resembled CLL at the immunogenetic level, displaying a more clonal profile and greater intraclonal diversification within the immunoglobulin (IG) genes compared to LC-MBL. LC-MBL, on the other hand, often exhibited oligoclonal characteristics with limited intraclonal diversification and fewer stereotyped BcR IG patterns.\nTranscriptomic analysis distinguished CLL from both HC-MBL and LC-MBL. While CLL showed upregulation in pathways such as Notch, p53, and BcR, HC-MBL and LC-MBL exhibited gene expression signatures associated with chronic inflammation and primary immunodeficiency, respectively. LC-MBL also demonstrated downregulation of pathways including BcR, FoxO, VEGF, and Wnt signaling compared to CLL.\nMoreover, recombinant monoclonal antibodies from MBL cases displayed weaker reactivity against a wide range of autoantigens and pathogens compared to CLL, which exhibited stronger recognition of a more limited set of antigens. Additionally, we found evidence of antigen-independent autonomous BcR signaling in MBL, even in LC-MBL cases.\nIn summary, our study highlights significant differences in BcR structure and function between MBL, particularly the LC-MBL subtype, and CLL. These disparities suggest distinct antigen exposure histories and maturation processes, underscoring the unique nature of LC-MBL in comparison to CLL.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":21,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMEA115610665","study_accession":"PRJEB75741","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"HC-MBL_6","description":"HC-MBL clonal B cells","sex":"female","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2025-04-14","attributes":[["ENA-CHECKLIST","ERC000011"],["organism","Homo sapiens"],["host scientific name","Centre for Research and Technology Hellas"],["collection date","2018"],["sex","Female"],["scientific_name","Homo sapiens"],["common name","human"],["cell_type","B cells"],["geographic location (region and locality)","Lombardy"],["tissue_type","Blood"],["geographic location (country and/or sea)","Italy"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"ERR13166313","experiment_accession":"ERX12537692","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"Oligo-dT","instrument_model":"NextSeq 550","read_count":73446522,"base_count":10633008210,"first_public":"2025-04-14","experiment_title":"NextSeq 550 sequencing"}],"labels":[]},{"sample_accession":"SAMEA115610666","study_accession":"PRJEB75741","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"HC-MBL_7","description":"HC-MBL clonal B cells","sex":"male","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2025-04-14","attributes":[["ENA-CHECKLIST","ERC000011"],["organism","Homo sapiens"],["host scientific name","Centre for Research and Technology Hellas"],["collection date","2018"],["sex","Male"],["scientific_name","Homo sapiens"],["common name","human"],["cell_type","B cells"],["geographic location (region and locality)","Lombardy"],["tissue_type","Blood"],["geographic location (country and/or sea)","Italy"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"ERR13166314","experiment_accession":"ERX12537693","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"Oligo-dT","instrument_model":"NextSeq 550","read_count":50658698,"base_count":4787234323,"first_public":"2025-04-14","experiment_title":"NextSeq 550 sequencing"}],"labels":[]},{"sample_accession":"SAMEA115610667","study_accession":"PRJEB75741","blood_class":"blo